workedExamples.question_text:
The connected network consists of a triangle and two edges leading to $T$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (2,0);
\coordinate (R) at (1,2);
\coordinate (S) at (3,2);
\coordinate (T) at (4.5,0.5);
\draw (P)--(Q)--(R)--(P);
\draw (R)--(S)--(T);
\foreach \v in {P,Q,R,S,T}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (P) node[below left] {$P$} (Q) node[below right] {$Q$} (R) node[above] {$R$} (S) node[above] {$S$} (T) node[right] {$T$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the smallest number of extra edges needed to permit an Eulerian circuit. State where to add them and explain why.

workedExamples.solution_text:
$\deg P=2$, $\deg Q=2$, $\deg R=3$, $\deg S=2$ and $\deg T=1$. The odd-degree vertices are $R$ and $T$.

An Eulerian circuit requires every degree to be even. Adding an edge between $R$ and $T$ raises both odd degrees by $1$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{solutionBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (2,0);
\coordinate (R) at (1,2);
\coordinate (S) at (3,2);
\coordinate (T) at (4.5,0.5);
\draw (P)--(Q)--(R)--(P);
\draw (R)--(S)--(T);
\draw[solutionBlue] (R) .. controls (2.5,0.7) and (3.3,0.3) .. (T);
\foreach \v in {P,Q,R,S,T}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (P) node[below left] {$P\ (2)$} (Q) node[below right] {$Q\ (2)$} (R) node[above] {$R\ (4)$} (S) node[above] {$S\ (2)$} (T) node[right] {$T\ (2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The blue edge is the new connection. The network remains connected and every degree is now even.

Zero extra edges would leave two odd vertices, so the smallest number is $1$, joining $R$ and $T$.

workedExamples.question_text:
In the Königsberg bridges problem, vertices represent land masses and edges represent bridges. Explain why no walk can cross all seven bridges exactly once. Then find the smallest number of extra bridges needed to permit an Eulerian trail, give one possible new connection and identify the resulting endpoints.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (U) at (0,2);
\coordinate (V) at (0,-2);
\coordinate (W) at (2,0);
\coordinate (X) at (5,0);
\draw (U) .. controls (0.3,0.9) and (1,0.2) .. (W);
\draw (U) .. controls (1.1,1.8) and (1.8,1.1) .. (W);
\draw (V) .. controls (0.3,-0.9) and (1,-0.2) .. (W);
\draw (V) .. controls (1.1,-1.8) and (1.8,-1.1) .. (W);
\draw (W)--(X);
\draw (U) .. controls (2,3) and (4.5,2) .. (X);
\draw (V) .. controls (2,-3) and (4.5,-2) .. (X);
\foreach \v in {U,V,W,X}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (U) node[left] {$U$} (V) node[left] {$V$} (W) node[above right] {$W$} (X) node[right] {$X$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
$\deg U=3$, $\deg V=3$, $\deg W=5$ and $\deg X=3$. All four land masses have odd degree.

At an intermediate land mass, arrival and departure use bridges in pairs. Only the start and finish can have an unpaired bridge, so a walk using every bridge exactly once permits at most two odd vertices. The original network has four, so no such walk exists.

Add one bridge between $U$ and $V$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{solutionBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\coordinate (U) at (0,2);
\coordinate (V) at (0,-2);
\coordinate (W) at (2,0);
\coordinate (X) at (5,0);
\draw (U) .. controls (0.3,0.9) and (1,0.2) .. (W);
\draw (U) .. controls (1.1,1.8) and (1.8,1.1) .. (W);
\draw (V) .. controls (0.3,-0.9) and (1,-0.2) .. (W);
\draw (V) .. controls (1.1,-1.8) and (1.8,-1.1) .. (W);
\draw (W)--(X);
\draw (U) .. controls (2,3) and (4.5,2) .. (X);
\draw (V) .. controls (2,-3) and (4.5,-2) .. (X);
\draw[solutionBlue] (U) .. controls (-1.3,1) and (-1.3,-1) .. (V);
\foreach \v in {U,V,W,X}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (U) node[above left] {$U\ (4)$} (V) node[below left] {$V\ (4)$} (W) node[above right] {$W\ (5)$} (X) node[right] {$X\ (3)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The blue bridge makes $U$ and $V$ even. The network is connected and only $W$ and $X$ remain odd, so an Eulerian trail exists between $W$ and $X$, in either direction.

The original network does not permit the walk, and one extra bridge does. The minimum is $1$.

