workedExamples.solution_text:
The network has two separate components. A walk along edges cannot travel from one triangle to the other, so there is no Eulerian circuit covering every edge, despite the even degrees.

One edge between $K$ and $M$ would connect the components, but it would give both vertices degree $3$. This would permit an open Eulerian trail, not an Eulerian circuit.

Add two separate edges between $K$ and $M$ instead.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{solutionBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (2,0);
\coordinate (L) at (1,1.8);
\coordinate (M) at (4,0);
\coordinate (N) at (6,0);
\coordinate (P) at (5,1.8);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(J);
\draw (M)--(N)--(P)--(M);
\draw[solutionBlue] (K)--(M);
\draw[solutionBlue] (K) .. controls (2.6,-0.9) and (3.4,-0.9) .. (M);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M,N,P}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J\ (2)$} (K) node[below, yshift=-12mm] {$K\ (4)$} (L) node[above] {$L\ (2)$} (M) node[below, yshift=-12mm] {$M\ (4)$} (N) node[below right] {$N\ (2)$} (P) node[above] {$P\ (2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The blue edges connect the components and raise both affected degrees to $4$. All degrees are even, so an Eulerian circuit exists.

Zero or one extra edge cannot satisfy both requirements. The smallest number is $2$.

