\coordinate (E) at (4.6,3.4);
\coordinate (F) at (4.8,1.2);
\draw (A)--(B);
\draw (B)--(C);
\draw (C)--(D);
\draw (D)--(A);
\draw (C)--(E);
\draw (E)--(F);
\draw (F)--(C);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (F) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below left] at (A) {$A\,(2)$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B\,(2)$};
\node[above] at (C) {$C\,(4)$};
\node[above left] at (D) {$D\,(2)$};
\node[above right] at (E) {$E\,(2)$};
\node[right] at (F) {$F\,(2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
3. **Count the vertices of odd degree**
Every degree is even, so there are no odd vertices.
4. **Apply the test for a circuit or a trail**
A connected network with no odd vertices has an Eulerian circuit, so the sweeper can cover every street once and return to $A$.

